<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Socialist Republic of Romania</h1><p class="st-meta">1947 CE – 1989 CE · capital Bucharest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Communist Romania, which under Nicolae Ceaușescu combined independence from Moscow with one of Eastern Europe's harshest dictatorships.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>With the Soviet army in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>, the communists took power step by step after 1945, and on 30 December 1947 King Michael I was forced to abdicate, ending the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. The Romanian People's Republic was proclaimed the same day, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gheorghe-gheorghiu-dej">Gheorghe Gheorghiu-Dej</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime nationalised industry, collectivised land and sent opponents to prisons and labour camps such as those on the Danube-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> Canal. Soviet troops left in 1958. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>, leader from 1965, renamed the country the Socialist Republic, refused to join the 1968 invasion of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and won Western favour, but built a personality cult, banned abortion in 1966 and imposed severe austerity in the 1980s to pay off foreign debt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Timișoara</a> in December 1989 spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>. The army turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Ceaușescu</a>, who was captured and executed with his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elena-ceausescu">Elena</a> on 25 December 1989 after a summary trial. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-salvation-front-romania">National Salvation Front</a> took power, founding post-communist <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/socialist-republic-of-romania.jpg" alt="Socialist Republic of Romania" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Various, see File History below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania_(1965%E2%80%931989).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1947 CE – 1989 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bucharest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gheorghe Gheorghiu-Dej, Nicolae Ceaușescu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism; Romanian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Romanian People's Republic, Communist Romania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=socialist-republic-of-romania" aria-label="Socialist Republic of Romania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5304 607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Socialist Republic of Romania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5624 863L5624 863M5624 863L5624 863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5624 863h0M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5624 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bucharest. <a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=socialist-republic-of-romania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
